course: biology
lesson: 214
title: Hedgehogs: spines, hibernation and how to help them
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A hedgehog wears about five thousand spines, and each one is a stiff, hollow hair.
explain: When it is scared, a ring of muscle pulls its skin tight like the string on a drawstring bag, and the hedgehog becomes a prickly ball that most animals give up on.
explain: At night it snuffles and grunts through gardens hunting beetles, worms, caterpillars and slugs, walking a couple of kilometres, and in winter, when food runs out, it hibernates in a nest of leaves.
explain: Hedgehogs are getting rarer in Britain, so small things matter: a hole in a fence, a ramp in a pond, a bonfire pile checked before it is lit, and water instead of milk.
fact: A hedgehog has about five thousand brown and cream spines, which are hollow hairs; it cannot shoot or throw them, and a muscle pulls its skin tight to roll it into a ball.
fact: Milk is bad for hedgehogs, which cannot digest it and can get very ill; a dish of water and meaty cat or dog food are safe.
fact: Hedgehogs hunt at night for beetles, worms, caterpillars and slugs, which makes them friends of gardeners, and one can walk a couple of kilometres a night.
fact: Hedgehogs are night animals, so one out in the daytime may be ill or in trouble, and it is best to call a wildlife rescue for advice.
fact: In winter hedgehogs hibernate: the body cools and the heart slows right down to save energy while food is short, and they must be fat enough in autumn to survive.
fact: Hedgehogs often crawl into piles of leaves and wood, so a bonfire pile should be checked before lighting or, better, built on the day.
fact: Hedgehogs travel through many gardens to find food and mates, so neighbours cut holes about the size of a CD in their fences to make hedgehog highways.
fact: Slug pellets can poison hedgehogs that eat poisoned slugs, and hedgehogs are getting rarer in Britain.
fact: Sometimes a hedgehog chews something with a new smell and spreads foamy spit all over its spines; this is called self-anointing, and scientists are still not sure why it does it.
fact: Hedgehogs snore, snuffle and grunt, and they got their name because they root about in hedges and grunt like pigs; their babies are called hoglets.
fact: Hedgehogs can swim, but they get stuck in ponds with steep sides, so a ramp or a few stones at the edge lets them climb out.
exercise: What are a hedgehog's spines made of? | Hollow, stiff hairs | Tiny bones | Fingernails | Thorns from bushes
exercise: Roughly how many spines does a hedgehog have? | About five thousand | About fifty | About five | About five million
exercise: Can a hedgehog shoot its spines at an enemy? | No, it rolls into a ball instead | Yes, like arrows from a bow, at anything near | Yes, but only in the cold of winter | Only the baby hedgehogs can do it
exercise: Why should you not give a hedgehog milk? | Hedgehogs cannot digest it and get ill | Milk makes their spines fall out | Milk is too cold for them | Hedgehogs only drink milk at Christmas
exercise: What is safe to put out for a hedgehog? | Water and meaty cat or dog food | A saucer of milk | Bread soaked in warm milk with a little sugar | Chocolate biscuits
exercise: Why do gardeners like hedgehogs? | They eat slugs, beetles and caterpillars | They dig up weeds | They water the plants at night with dew from their spines | They scare away the rain
exercise: About how far can a hedgehog walk in a night? | A couple of kilometres | Only a couple of metres | About a hundred kilometres | It never leaves its nest
exercise: Why can a hedgehog out in the daytime be a worry? | They are night animals, so it may be ill | It means it is about to rain | It will turn into a different animal | It has forgotten where it lives and is looking for home
exercise: What happens to a hedgehog's body when it hibernates? | It cools down and its heart slows right down | It gets hotter and its heart races to keep it warm all winter | It sheds all its spines | It grows a thick winter fur coat
exercise: Why must hedgehogs eat a lot in autumn? | To be fat enough to survive hibernation | To grow longer, sharper spines for winter | To feed their babies milk all through the winter | To get heavy so the wind cannot blow them away
exercise: Why should a bonfire pile be checked before lighting? | A hedgehog may be sleeping inside | Hedgehogs like to light fires | The wood may be too wet | Hedgehogs steal the matches
exercise: What is a hedgehog highway? | Small holes in fences linking gardens | A little road built only for hedgehogs | A long line of hedges planted along a motorway | A tunnel under the sea to another country
exercise: Why are slug pellets dangerous for hedgehogs? | Hedgehogs eat poisoned slugs | The pellets stick to their spines | Hedgehogs think the pellets are eggs | The pellets make too much noise
exercise: What is self-anointing? | Spreading foamy spit on its spines after chewing a new smell | Washing in a pond | Rolling in leaves and grass to make a warm nest for the winter | Licking its babies clean
exercise: Where does the name hedgehog come from? | It roots in hedges and grunts like a pig | It is half hedge and half pig | It was first seen on a pig farm, hiding in the hedge | It eats hedges like a hog
exercise: What are baby hedgehogs called? | Hoglets | Piglets | Kits | Cubs
exercise: How can you make a garden pond safe for hedgehogs? | Put a ramp or some stones at the edge | Fill it with milk | Put a heavy lid on it all year round | Paint the sides blue so hedgehogs can see the water
test: «left the hedgehog a bowl of milk, he'll love it» What is right? | Milk makes hedgehogs ill; leave water and meaty cat food instead | Milk is the perfect hedgehog food, especially cold from the fridge | Hedgehogs only drink warm milk | Add sugar to the milk for energy
test: Sonic the Hedgehog is blue, runs faster than sound and spins into a ball to attack. What is true of real hedgehogs? | They are brown, walk a couple of kilometres a night and roll into a ball to defend | They are blue and run faster than cars on a motorway | They spin into a ball to attack foxes and badgers | They roll only to go downhill fast
test: «hedgehog mum and 4 hoglets under the shed, how many legs» | 20 | 16 | 10 | 24
test: «3 hedgehogs and a fox in the garden, how many ears, no tusks no trunks» | 8 | 6 | 4 | 16
test: «hedgehog wandering about at lunchtime, looks wobbly, it's fine yeah» What should you do? | Ask a wildlife rescue for advice, as it may be ill | Leave it, they love the daytime and come out to sunbathe | Give it a bowl of milk | Chase it back under the hedge
test: «dad's had the bonfire pile up for 3 weeks, lighting it tonight» What should he do first? | Check it carefully for hedgehogs, or rebuild it on the day | Pour petrol on it to scare hedgehogs out | Nothing, hedgehogs never go in piles | Light it fast so hedgehogs run away
test: «my mate says hedgehogs fire their spikes at dogs» What is right? | No, they cannot throw spines; they roll into a ball | Yes, up to three metres | Yes, but only at cats | No, they bite hard with their spines when a dog comes too close
test: What would happen if every garden on a street had a solid fence with no gaps? | Hedgehogs could not travel between gardens to find food and mates | Hedgehogs would get bigger | Hedgehogs would learn to climb fences | Nothing, hedgehogs stay in one garden
test: «weird, hedgehog chewing my shoe and frothing all over its back, has it got rabies» What is likely? | It is self-anointing, which hedgehogs often do | It has rabies and must be taken away at once | It is making a nest with spit and bits of shoe | It is trying to eat the shoe
test: What would happen if a hedgehog went into winter thin and hungry? | It might not survive hibernation | It would just hibernate for longer | It would stay awake all winter and be fine | It would grow much fatter while asleep
test: «hedgehog fell in the pond, it'll just drown» What is right? | Hedgehogs can swim, but need a ramp or stones to climb out | Hedgehogs sink like stones as soon as they touch the water | Hedgehogs breathe underwater | Hedgehogs never go near water
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the spines of the hedgehog? | They are hollow hairs, some five thousand in number, which cannot be discharged | They are bones which may be projected at an assailant over some distance | They are thorns acquired from hedges | They number fewer than fifty
exam: Identify the principal hazard posed to the hedgehog by a bonfire constructed some weeks before ignition. | A hedgehog may have made its nest within the pile | The smoke attracts hedgehogs from afar | Hedgehogs are drawn to warmth and will light fires | The pile prevents hedgehogs from hibernating
exam: It is given that a hedgehog is observed abroad in daylight and moving unsteadily. Ascertain the appropriate course of action. | Advice should be sought from a wildlife rescue, the animal being probably unwell | The animal should be given a saucer of milk | The animal should be chased back into the hedge | No action is warranted, as hedgehogs prefer daylight
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the hibernation of hedgehogs. | The body cools and the heart slows to conserve energy, sufficient fat being required beforehand | The body warms and the heart accelerates throughout the winter | Hibernation requires the animal to fast throughout the autumn | Hedgehogs remain fully active throughout the winter
